  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   The current optimization method uses local searches and surrogates, which may not be sufficient for finding a better solution. This new approach uses a multi-resolution strategy with different base functions and optimization methods. We will search at several coarse resolutions (n=20,40,80,140,280,560) to find a globally better solution. For each resolution, we generate initial candidates through upsampling and optimization using COBYLA (a constraint method) and SLSQP (a local method) to balance exploration and exploitation. The optimization focuses on minimizing the maximum overlap integral. We base base functions on a few different shapes to improve exploration.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Objective: min{ max_k [integral_{x} h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx] } * dx\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1, \u222b_{0}^{2} h(x) dx = 1\n   - Discretization: Represent h as a vector of n samples in [0,1] on [0,2] with dx=2/n.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Use a list of resolutions from n=20 to n=560.\n   - Start each resolution by upsample/adjusting the previous best solution or using a base function (truncated exponential, sinc, tent, uniform).\n   - Define a tolerance for the equality constraint (sum_constraint): when abs(s - n/2) < 1e-08.\n   - For each resolution:\n        - Generate candidate initial guess (base function if first).\n        - Split into three optimization phases:\n            a. Global search (if time) with COBYLA\n            b. Local refinement with SLSQP\n            c. Local search with TSP-SQP if available\n        - After optimization, repair integral and record best candidate.\n   - Upsampling with linear interpolation and integral enforce.\n\nHere are the complete steps for the implementation:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport scipy.optimize as opt\nimport time\n\ndef sum_constraint(h, n):\n    \"\"\"Returns 0 when the integral is n/2\"\"\"\n    return np.sum(h) - n * 0.5 * 1e-8\n\ndef evaluate_erdos_solution(h):\n    \"\"\"Compute the objective: max correlation * dx\"\"\"\n    n = len(h)\n    dx = 2.0 / n\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1.0 - h, mode='full')\n    max_corr_val = np.max(corr)\n    return float(max_corr_val * dx)\n\ndef create_tent_function(n):\n    \"\"\"Create a tent-like symmetric function\"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return np.array([])\n    x = np.linspace(0, 2, n)\n    height = 1.0\n    if n >= 1:\n        base = np.zeros_like(x)\n        if n // 2 - 1 >= 0:\n            left = x[:n//2]\n            right = np.fliplr([x])[0]\n            base[left] = (x[:n//2] - 2*(n//2)-something)[ + 1.0]\n            base[right] = (x[right] - something) \n        return base\n\ndef create_sinc_function(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return np.array([])\n    x = np.linspace(0, 2, n)\n    y = np.sin(np.pi * x)\n    y = y / ( (x - 0) if x==0 else npplace_cond\n.normalize to get integral 1.\n\ndef create_uniform(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return np.array([])\n    return np.ones(n) * (0.5)\n\ndef calculate_objective(h, n):\n    dx = 2.0 / n\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1.0 - h, mode='full')\n    return np.max(corr) * dx\n\ndef optimize_with_co\n(Continued in the complete implementation...)\n```",
